u.s surveillance satellites detected a  mysterious signal in the 1960s  flashes of gamma rays the highest energy  form of light  were expected from nuclear tests on  earth  but not from deep in space soviet and  u.s researchers joined  forces to investigate these powerful  bursts  using a variety of space missions  including helios  a spacecraft that orbited the sun and  the venera probes which  traverse the inner solar system most of  the bursts hailed from massive stars  exploding in supernovae  but in 1979 scientists encountered an  extraordinary flash  from a galaxy near the milky way by 1992  researchers had a theory for what could  have created such a powerful signal  a magnetically amped-up neutron star  they gave it a name magnetar  neutron stars are the densest form of  matter  they form when the core of a star  implodes and shrinks to the size of a  city  this amplifies the star's pre-existing  magnetic field  but the flash captured in 1979 pointed  to something even more magnetic than a  normal neutron star  scientists proposed that during the  early life of some neutron stars  their molten guts churn enough to create  dynamos  swirling currents that create powerful  magnetic fields  a magnetar's magnetic field is about a  trillion times clingier than  refrigerator magnets  and a thousand times more powerful than  an average neutron  [Music]  researchers have been waiting for the  perfect opportunity to study these  extreme neutron stars  that event finally came last year on  april 15  2020 when sensors in space and on earth  detected an unusual gamma-ray burst from  sculptor  a nearby galaxy the brevity of this  signal  mere milliseconds suggested that it came  from an eruption  on the surface of a magnetar just as  movements in the earth's crust cause  earthquakes  neutron stars can rupture in magnetars  starquakes result from grinding and  magnetic stresses in its hard  outer crust the rupture releases an  explosion of charged particles or  plasma that in turn generates a beam of  gamma-ray photons  as the first giant flare in 16 years  this event allowed researchers to use  recent technology  to answer long-standing questions about  magnetars  they calculated the strength of the  flare and found it packed a hundred  thousand years of the sun's energy in  its quick  burst based on the timing of the  gamma-ray burst  they were also able to confirm that the  plasma was let loose near the pole of  the magnetar  in the wake of the burst researchers  have searched for overlooked magnetar  events  in astronomical back catalogs they found  three additional bursts from galaxies  near the milky way  bumping the total number of recorded  magnetar flares to seven  researchers think this means that these  flares are more common across the  universe than previously thought  and that it's relatively easy for  neutron stars to be born as magnetars  it also suggests that distant magnetar  eruptions  might explain other mystifying phenomena  such as fast radio bursts  scientists are eager and ready to detect  the next burst that will send a  shockwave  through the field of astronomy  [Music]  you
 

